WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.875
SPEAKER: We've covered
using Cloud monitoring

00:00:01.875 --> 00:00:02.610
in your projects.

00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:05.310
But what if you're responsible
for managing many projects,

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:07.680
and you need to unify
monitoring across them?

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:09.900
And how can you control
access to monitoring data

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:11.250
from many projects?

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:14.310
Today, we'll look at Cloud
monitoring metrics scopes,

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:16.560
and learn how to use them
to monitor multiple Cloud

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:17.500
projects.

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:20.250
Welcome to Engineering for
Reliability with Google Cloud.

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:26.020
Let's start with
a simple example.

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:28.120
How can you make it easier
to view monitoring data

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:31.230
for multiple projects?

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:33.360
If I go to monitoring
settings for my project,

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:35.550
I can see that my current
metrics scope only

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:38.610
has a single project in it,
the one I'm currently viewing.

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:41.730
I can add others, but what's
actually happening here?

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:46.090
Let's have a look at metrics
scopes and how they work.

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:48.010
When you create a
Google Cloud project,

00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:50.290
that project hosts
a metrics scope

00:00:50.290 --> 00:00:53.110
and becomes the scoping
project for that scope.

00:00:53.110 --> 00:00:56.170
It stores the alerts,
uptime checks, dashboards,

00:00:56.170 --> 00:00:59.440
and monitoring groups that
you configure for the scope.

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:02.290
For example, if you create
a staging project and then

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:04.360
access monitoring, you
can see the metrics

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:06.940
for the resources in
the staging project.

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:09.190
This happens for every
project you create.

00:01:09.190 --> 00:01:12.010
Each project creates a
metrics scope for itself,

00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:15.120
and hosts monitoring
configuration for itself.

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:17.310
But what if you want to
centralize how that data is

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:19.050
stored and how it's accessed?

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:22.200
You can add multiple projects
to an existing scope.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:25.530
Now, monitoring data for
all projects in that scope

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:27.077
will be visible.

00:01:27.077 --> 00:01:29.160
This will let you do things
like create dashboards

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:31.740
showing resources from all
the projects in the scope,

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:34.020
or alerting policies
that apply to resources

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:38.068
in multiple projects as long as
they're in the metrics scope.

00:01:38.068 --> 00:01:40.110
Note that the recommended
approach for production

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:42.900
deployments is to create a
dedicated project to host

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:45.630
monitoring configuration
data and use its metrics

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:47.820
scope to set up monitoring
for the projects that

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:49.740
have actual resources in them.

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:52.440
This way, should a project
not be necessary anymore

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:54.810
and get deleted, the
monitoring configuration

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:57.750
for all the other projects
won't be impacted.

00:01:57.750 --> 00:01:58.950
What about access?

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:01.080
While in this
configuration, you just

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:03.000
must have appropriate
IAM permissions

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:04.900
to access monitoring data.

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:07.350
For example, a user would
need to have the right role

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:10.020
in the monitoring project
to be able to see monitoring

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:12.030
data for both projects.

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:14.970
A user who only has access
to the staging project

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:16.890
would not be able to
see any monitoring data

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:18.960
for the production project.

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:21.060
Now that you understand
what metrics scopes are

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:22.860
and what to use
them for, let's have

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:26.517
a look at how to set them
up in Cloud monitoring.

00:02:26.517 --> 00:02:27.975
Let's start in our
staging project.

00:02:30.548 --> 00:02:32.340
We'll go to Compute
Engine, and see that we

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:33.630
have a single VM running.

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:40.510
We can go to monitoring, and see
that monitoring data for the VM

00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:43.750
is being shown.

00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:45.500
For example, on the
infrastructure summary

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:46.400
dashboard.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:52.480
Let's now go to our
monitoring project,

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:54.700
and confirm that there
aren't any compute instances

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:55.510
running in it.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:01.130
We also don't have the
out-of-the-box compute

00:03:01.130 --> 00:03:03.980
dashboards because we don't have
any resources running in this

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:04.700
project.

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:06.860
Let's now add our
staging project

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:10.060
to be monitored by
this metrics scope.

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:15.700
We can do that in monitoring
settings by adding a project,

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:18.790
selecting the project
we want to add,

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:21.220
and confirming that we
want to use this monitoring

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:24.010
project as the scoping
project rather than creating

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:24.700
a new scope.

00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:31.340
Once this operation is
complete, monitoring data

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:35.150
from the staging project will be
visible in this metrics scope.

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:38.030
We can confirm that by going
to the VM instances dashboard,

00:03:38.030 --> 00:03:39.283
for example.

00:03:39.283 --> 00:03:40.700
From here, we can
see more details

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:43.040
about the instance being
monitored, even though it's

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:45.510
running in another project.

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:48.110
So that's how we can use the
Cloud Console to configure

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:50.000
metrics scopes and
collect monitoring

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:53.490
data for multiple projects.

00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:56.270
So that's how to configure
metrics scopes in the UI.

00:03:56.270 --> 00:03:58.340
But how can you figure
out where your project is

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:00.770
being monitored, or where the
monitoring data you can see

00:04:00.770 --> 00:04:02.540
in your project is coming from?

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:04.760
Let's have a look.

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:06.740
Now that we have added
our staging project

00:04:06.740 --> 00:04:08.810
to our monitoring
metrics scope, let's

00:04:08.810 --> 00:04:11.090
see how we can navigate
between the two.

00:04:11.090 --> 00:04:13.430
We can see information about
our current metrics scope

00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:15.950
here on the monitoring overview
page for the monitoring

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:17.000
project.

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:20.029
If we click on that, we can
see that the monitoring project

00:04:20.029 --> 00:04:21.920
is monitoring
metrics for resources

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:26.180
running in the project itself
and in our staging project.

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:30.330
We can go there
directly from here.

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:33.570
In the staging project,
we can do the same thing,

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:36.450
and see that this project is
monitoring metrics for itself,

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:40.140
and that it is being monitored
by our monitoring project.

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:41.730
And we can go back
to that project

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:44.940
simply by clicking on that link.

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:47.240
So that's how you can navigate
between metrics scopes

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:51.320
and monitored projects
in Cloud monitoring.

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:53.810
Navigating metrics scopes
in the UI is easy enough.

00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:56.240
But how can you ensure that,
for example, new projects

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:58.640
are added to your Production
Monitoring configuration?

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:00.770
You can use the API
to automate that.

00:05:00.770 --> 00:05:03.410
Let's have a look.

00:05:03.410 --> 00:05:07.550
Let's start in
monitoring settings,

00:05:07.550 --> 00:05:10.520
and confirm that this
particular metrics scope is only

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:12.650
monitoring this project,
which is the scoping

00:05:12.650 --> 00:05:15.050
project for this metrics scope.

00:05:15.050 --> 00:05:17.240
Now let's go to our API.

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:20.030
We'll use the Create method
for metrics scopes projects

00:05:20.030 --> 00:05:21.840
which takes two arguments.

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:24.140
First one is the parent,
which is the metrics

00:05:24.140 --> 00:05:27.930
scope that we want to add
our stage and project to.

00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:30.280
The second is our
project itself,

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:32.490
which is a monitored
project resource.

00:05:32.490 --> 00:05:34.110
The name field
includes a reference

00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:36.720
to both the staging project
and the metrics scope

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:39.150
we're adding this project to.

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:42.690
Let's execute this request,
authenticate, and confirm

00:05:42.690 --> 00:05:44.770
that the operation is done.

00:05:44.770 --> 00:05:47.610
We can now go back to monitoring
settings for our monitoring

00:05:47.610 --> 00:05:50.760
project, refresh the
page, and confirm

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:52.320
that the staging
project has been

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:54.210
added to this metrics scope.

00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:56.220
So that's how you can
use the Cloud monitoring

00:05:56.220 --> 00:06:00.980
API to manage how projects are
monitored by metrics scopes.

00:06:00.980 --> 00:06:02.340
Thanks for joining me today.

00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:04.340
We covered how metrics
scopes lets you configure

00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:07.790
monitoring across projects, and
how to manage them in the UI

00:06:07.790 --> 00:06:09.170
and using the API.

00:06:09.170 --> 00:06:11.090
I hope this helps you
standardize and automate

00:06:11.090 --> 00:06:13.520
your monitoring, run
more reliable services,

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:14.750
and keep your users happy.

00:06:14.750 --> 00:06:16.350
Thanks so much for watching.

00:06:16.350 --> 00:06:18.530
Don't forget to like and
subscribe to never miss out

00:06:18.530 --> 00:06:21.290
on more Engineering for
Reliability with Google Cloud.

00:06:21.290 --> 00:06:23.170
See you soon.

